# Icon System Reference

This project uses **font-based icons** organized into themed icon sets. Each theme maps to a primary icon font. The base `ld` (Living Design) icons are always available.

## Theme → Icon Font Mapping

Each theme resolves to exactly **one** primary icon font, and that mapping is
generated — `node scripts/ld/cli.mjs icons` with no arguments lists every font with its icon
count, and `node scripts/ld/cli.mjs icons <theme-name> <query>` resolves a theme to its font for
you. Don't copy the mapping into code.

The 1:1 relationship is the part worth knowing: the `Icon` component uses
`useThemeIconPrefix()`, which returns only the active theme's primary font
prefix — it never renders `ld`-prefixed icons. Use
`getThemePrimaryIconFont(theme)` / `getThemeIconCssPrefix(theme)` from
`@walmart/ld-kit/theming` when you need the value programmatically. The
`_loadFonts` array is internal to CSS font loading — never iterate it in UI code.

## How to Find Available Icons

```bash
node scripts/ld/cli.mjs icons                        # every font, with counts
node scripts/ld/cli.mjs icons <query>                # search every font, e.g. `icons cart`
node scripts/ld/cli.mjs icons <font-or-theme> <query>  # scope to one font; a theme name works too
```

This reads the same manifest `iconManager`'s `listIcons` / `searchIcons` read at
runtime, so a name it returns is guaranteed to render. **Look the name up rather
than recalling one** — a plausible-sounding icon name that does not exist is the
most common icon defect, and it is invisible until the glyph fails to paint.

Public codepoint exports live at `@walmart/ld-kit/icons/<font>`.

## Programmatic Access

All icon code should import from `@walmart/ld-kit/utils/iconManager` — one
import surface for both theme→font helpers and manifest queries:

```ts
import {
  // Theme → font (re-exported from Theming)
  getThemePrimaryIconFont,
  getThemeIconCssPrefix,
  useThemeIconPrefix,
  // Manifest queries
  listIconFonts,
  getIconFontInfo,
  listIcons,
  hasIcon,
  searchIcons,
  getIconsForTheme,
  hasIconForTheme,
  getCommonIcons,
} from '@walmart/ld-kit/utils/iconManager';

getIconsForTheme('Walmart');          // string[] of icon names for the wcp font
hasIcon('sams-club', 'Cart');          // true
searchIcons('wcp', 'chevron');         // ['ChevronDown', 'ChevronLeft', ...]
getCommonIcons();                      // names present in every font
```

Use these helpers for any "what icons exist for theme X" question — they
read `reference/fonts/icon-manifest.json` directly, so they stay in sync with
the next Airtable pull. `getCommonIcons()` returns the names present in every
font, which is the safe set when the active theme is not known.

## Usage

### Pre-made icon components (preferred)

Import from `@walmart/ld-kit`:

```tsx
import {SearchIcon, StarIcon, SettingsIcon, PlusIcon, CheckIcon, XIcon, InfoIcon, AlertTriangleIcon, ChevronUpIcon}
  from '@walmart/ld-kit';

<IconButton a11yLabel="Search"><SearchIcon /></IconButton>
<SpotIcon color="brand"><StarIcon /></SpotIcon>
```

Available pre-made wrappers: `SearchIcon`, `ChevronUpIcon`, `SettingsIcon` (Gear), `StarIcon`, `CheckIcon`, `PlusIcon`, `InfoIcon` (InfoCircle), `AlertTriangleIcon` (Warning), `XIcon` (Close).

These components are **theme-aware** — they automatically resolve the correct icon font CSS class based on the active theme (e.g. `wcp wcp-Search` for Walmart, `sc sc-Search` for Sam's Club). Font loading is handled by the theming system (`loadThemeFonts()` in `@walmart/ld-kit/theming`), no manual font injection is needed.

### Generic icon component (any icon name)

```tsx
import {Icon} from '@walmart/ld-kit';

<Icon name="Cart" />
<Icon name="Heart" style={{fontSize: 24}} />
```

Props: `name` (required), `size` (`"small"` | `"medium"` | `"large"`), `a11yLabel`, `style`, `className`, `title`. The component reads the current theme from the `data-ld-theme` attribute and renders with the appropriate font CSS prefix.

### SVG icons (theme-independent)

A small number of SVG-only icons have no font equivalent: `WPlusIcon`, `SparkIcon`, `HourglassIcon`, `ShieldCheckIcon`. All other icons have been migrated to the font-based `Icon` system, both available from `@walmart/ld-kit`.

## Rules

- **NEVER use an emoji or a Unicode glyph as an icon** (🔍 ⚙️ ✅ ❌ ⚠️ ℹ️ ✦ ⌄ ⓘ …), anywhere in
  component code or UI text. They are not part of Living Design, render
  differently on every platform, cannot be themed, and are announced
  unpredictably by screen readers. If no icon matches the concept, use the
  closest one or omit it — never fall back to an emoji.
- **Look the name up** with `node scripts/ld/cli.mjs icons` instead of recalling it; use `Icon`
  with a manifest name, never a raw codepoint.
- **Check the active theme** before using commerce-specific icons (`Cart`,
  `AddToCart`, `GroceryBag`). These exist in `wcp`, `sams-club` and `bodega`,
  but not in `ld`.
- **Use the pre-made wrappers** when one exists — they carry consistent sizing
  and accessibility defaults.
- **Use `IconButton`** for interactive icon-only buttons, never a bare `<button>`
  with an icon inside. Use `SpotIcon` for a decorative circular treatment.
- **Do not hardcode `font-family`** — the theme system loads the right icon font
  via `loadThemeFonts()`.
- **Accessibility**: icons inside interactive elements are `aria-hidden` and the
  parent carries the label; standalone meaningful icons need their own. Full
  directive in `node scripts/ld/cli.mjs rule a11y`.
